# Line endings are a property of the REPOSITORY, not of whoever cloned it.
#
# Without this file, LF-correctness depends on `core.autocrlf` being set on each
# machine that touches the repo. It happens to be `true` on the Windows dev box
# (bggr-k), which is why the object store is clean today — but that is a local
# setting, and the repo is cloned onto Linux hosts (the Brisbane VPS, tnt-open-c1)
# and would be cloned by CI or a fresh install with no such guarantee.
#
# This matters concretely: a CR in a shebang breaks direct execution on Linux
# (paid for on 2026-07-25 — a drive-sync module had to be reinstalled
# LF-normalised because a CR in its shebang broke exec). Shell scripts here are
# rsynced to a production VPS and run there.

# Normalise everything text-like to LF in the object store, detect binary by heuristic.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that MUST be LF regardless — they are executed on Linux.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.cron      text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
*.timer     text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf

# Windows-only helpers keep CRLF, or cmd.exe mis-parses them.
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# Never touch these.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.zip       binary
*.gz        binary
*.db        binary
*.sqlite    binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
